WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:06.430
This video is not an essay, it is a raw nerve.

00:00:06.430 --> 00:00:11.320
Contagion is a 2011 film directed by Steven
Soderbergh, starring an ensemble cast including

00:00:11.320 --> 00:00:16.520
Marion Cotillard, Matt Damon, Laurence Fishburne,
Jude Law, Gwyneth Paltrow, and Kate Winslet.

00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:22.440
The film revolves around the origin, contraction,
spread, and cure of the fictional MEV-1 virus,

00:00:22.440 --> 00:00:26.800
a highly contagious, aggressive, and fatal
strain of hybrid bat and pig flu.

00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:31.090
The initial patient, Beth Emhoff, played by
Gwyneth Paltrow, contracts the virus in Macau

00:00:31.090 --> 00:00:35.590
after shaking hands with a casino chef who
has recently handled an infected pig.

00:00:35.590 --> 00:00:39.780
She spreads the virus to several other people
in the casino after they handle objects that

00:00:39.780 --> 00:00:43.980
she’s touched, such as gambling chips, a
martini glass, and her cell phone.

00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:48.879
An important aspect of the film is that the
fictional virus is highly transmissible via

00:00:48.879 --> 00:00:53.070
fomites, which are objects that an infected
person has touched after touching their mouth

00:00:53.070 --> 00:00:57.860
or nose, coughing or sneezing on the object,
or otherwise leaving infectious residue on

00:00:57.860 --> 00:01:00.760
an otherwise inert, non-biological object.

00:01:00.760 --> 00:01:05.239
A local waiter who handled her glass returns
home, infecting members of his family before

00:01:05.239 --> 00:01:09.810
wandering into traffic in a fever-induced
delirium where he is struck by a vehicle and

00:01:09.810 --> 00:01:10.810
killed.

00:01:10.810 --> 00:01:14.999
A Japanese businessman who shared chips with
her returns to Tokyo where he falls ill rapidly,

00:01:14.999 --> 00:01:19.340
dying suddenly of a seizure on a crowded bus,
infecting several bystanders who touch him

00:01:19.340 --> 00:01:21.109
or handrails that he touched.

00:01:21.109 --> 00:01:25.789
A Ukranian model who handled Beth’s phone
flies to London where her symptoms also escalate

00:01:25.789 --> 00:01:30.259
rapidly while she transmits the disease to
others via handling portfolios and riding

00:01:30.259 --> 00:01:31.599
in a cab.

00:01:31.599 --> 00:01:35.750
Beth returns to America where she infects
several people in Chicago, first her ex lover

00:01:35.750 --> 00:01:40.070
Jon who contracts it when they have sex while
she is on layover, and a bartender at the

00:01:40.070 --> 00:01:44.090
airport who handles her credit card, before
flying to Minneapolis where she infects the

00:01:44.090 --> 00:01:47.439
coworker who drives her home from the airport
and her son Clark.

00:01:47.439 --> 00:01:51.520
A day or two later Beth’s husband, Mitch,
played by Matt Damon, picks up Clark from

00:01:51.520 --> 00:01:54.270
school after Clark begins to exhibit a fever.

00:01:54.270 --> 00:01:58.049
While Beth and Mitch are talking in the kitchen
Beth suddenly has a seizure.

00:01:58.049 --> 00:02:01.950
Mitch rushes her to the hospital, leaving
Clark with a babysitter, but Beth’s condition

00:02:01.950 --> 00:02:06.459
continues to worsen, she fails to respond
to treatment, and she dies.

00:02:06.459 --> 00:02:10.399
As a stunned Mitch is driving home he gets
a call from the babysitter that Clark has

00:02:10.399 --> 00:02:13.519
possibly had a seizure and might not be breathing.

00:02:13.519 --> 00:02:18.409
Mitch tells her to call 911 immediately, but
before anyone can get there Clark is already

00:02:18.409 --> 00:02:19.409
dead.

00:02:19.409 --> 00:02:23.310
From there the story expands to encompass
the doctors, politicians, reporters, hucksters,

00:02:23.310 --> 00:02:27.220
and ordinary people who are swept up in an
all-encompassing pandemic that threatens to

00:02:27.220 --> 00:02:30.360
kill a quarter of the global population.

00:02:30.360 --> 00:02:35.439
The movie is an incredibly tense hundred minutes
of society pushed to its breaking points,

00:02:35.439 --> 00:02:41.520
not as a fantastical disintegration into wastelands
of leather-clad murder gangs or a zombie apocalypse,

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:45.720
but one rooted in the historical reality of
epidemics.

00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:47.550
And I can’t stop watching it.

00:02:47.550 --> 00:02:51.980
I have watched Contagion over fifteen times
in the last two weeks.

00:02:51.980 --> 00:02:55.909
Several days I’ve just watched it on repeat
two or three times.

00:02:55.909 --> 00:02:56.930
And I'm not alone.

00:02:56.930 --> 00:03:01.569
According to Netflix it is, at the time of
writing, the second most watched thing in

00:03:01.569 --> 00:03:02.569
Canada.

00:03:02.569 --> 00:03:05.090
For weeks it has sat in the top ten.

00:03:05.090 --> 00:03:10.159
Unlike many similar films, such as the 1995
film Outbreak starring Dustin Hoffman, the

00:03:10.159 --> 00:03:15.310
film is not about any one person, and there
is no singular twist of victory.

00:03:15.310 --> 00:03:17.920
Rather it is an example of sociological storytelling.

00:03:17.920 --> 00:03:21.780
It’s about the systems and networks that
these characters exist within, and how they

00:03:21.780 --> 00:03:26.620
both influence and are influenced by those
systems, and what happens when those systems

00:03:26.620 --> 00:03:29.319
are placed under tremendous strain.

00:03:29.319 --> 00:03:34.069
Kate Winslet plays Dr. Erin Mears, a front
line worker for the CDC who is sent to Wisconsin

00:03:34.069 --> 00:03:37.569
to track the transmission of the virus and
contain its spread.

00:03:37.569 --> 00:03:41.930
Half way through the film she catches the
virus herself, and then her condition worsens,

00:03:41.930 --> 00:03:43.650
and then she dies.

00:03:43.650 --> 00:03:44.650
It is unceremonious.

00:03:44.650 --> 00:03:50.609
It is not foreshadowed or paid off because
it is not poetic, because pandemics are not

00:03:50.609 --> 00:03:55.590
poetic and don’t have a tight arc or an
eye for narrative fulfillment.

00:03:55.590 --> 00:04:02.340
It doesn’t have meaning, the only meaning
is in how we choose to respond.

00:04:02.340 --> 00:04:08.239
Because this is sociological the movie doesn’t
end when doctor Ally Hextall develops a vaccine.

00:04:08.239 --> 00:04:12.439
What would be the singular victory moment
in most films is instead the beginning of

00:04:12.439 --> 00:04:18.090
a slow, painful march back to stability as
first the vaccine needs to be mass produced,

00:04:18.090 --> 00:04:21.340
and then distributed to billions of people
worldwide.

00:04:21.340 --> 00:04:26.000
It is a dangerous task that needs to be tightly
controlled as it requires access to the isolated

00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:29.610
virus and thus is very slow to ramp up.

00:04:29.610 --> 00:04:34.870
The film trudges through the immense societal
tension that is created when there is a cure,

00:04:34.870 --> 00:04:39.980
but it will take over a year to make and distribute
enough for everyone, a situation that lays

00:04:39.980 --> 00:04:42.380
bare every societal privilege.

00:04:42.380 --> 00:04:46.990
Dr. Orantes, played by Marion Cotillard, is
kidnapped and held ransom for the vaccine

00:04:46.990 --> 00:04:52.000
by Chinese villagers who are keenly aware
that in the priority of global politics the

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:56.530
poor, the rural, and the non-white are at
the very back of the line.

00:04:56.530 --> 00:05:00.000
They are terrorists, but they’re not wrong,
just desperate.

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:04.930
They are at the back of the line, and the
government throws them under the bus anyway.

00:05:04.930 --> 00:05:09.819
Despite the existence of a vaccine Mitch continues
to keep his teenage daughter, Jory, under

00:05:09.819 --> 00:05:14.500
aggressive quarantine out of legitimate fear
of the disease that has been amplified to

00:05:14.500 --> 00:05:20.250
paranoia by the trauma of losing Beth and
Clark, the survivor’s guilt of being naturally

00:05:20.250 --> 00:05:25.430
immune, and the uncertainty of whether his
daughter would share that immunity or not.

00:05:25.430 --> 00:05:31.710
In December 2019 the coronavirus COVID-19
was identified by doctors in the city of Wuhan.

00:05:31.710 --> 00:05:35.949
Over the course of January and February the
spread of the virus began to be identified

00:05:35.949 --> 00:05:40.419
in South Korea, Japan, and Italy and, gradually,
most of the rest of the world.

00:05:40.419 --> 00:05:44.530
The disease itself is not exceptionally lethal
when compared to epidemics such as the Black

00:05:44.530 --> 00:05:48.889
Death in the mid 14th century or the spread
of Smallpox through indiginous populations

00:05:48.889 --> 00:05:54.789
following contact with Europeans in the 16th
and 17th centuries, but, first of all, “better

00:05:54.789 --> 00:06:00.300
than the black death” is a pretty bad standard,
and second on a global scale a mortality rate

00:06:00.300 --> 00:06:05.990
of 1-2 percent in an unchecked pandemic still
means, in absolute terms, millions and millions

00:06:05.990 --> 00:06:07.550
of preventable deaths.

00:06:07.550 --> 00:06:12.550
This is compounded by the strain that mass
illness, even one that is not terribly lethal,

00:06:12.550 --> 00:06:18.539
inherently places on an already strained society:
crowding healthcare systems, disrupting infrastructure,

00:06:18.539 --> 00:06:23.759
and forcing people to choose between working
while ill, and thus infecting others, or losing

00:06:23.759 --> 00:06:24.789
their jobs.

00:06:24.789 --> 00:06:29.370
A low mortality rate is often the result of
adequate care, but the quality of care goes

00:06:29.370 --> 00:06:35.010
down as the number of severely ill goes up,
as the number of infected healthcare workers

00:06:35.010 --> 00:06:40.600
reduces the number of people qualified and
capable of administering that care.

00:06:40.600 --> 00:06:44.830
This, in turn, has a knock on effect where
unrelated illnesses and injuries become more

00:06:44.830 --> 00:06:45.830
dangerous.

00:06:45.830 --> 00:06:49.340
A heart attack or broken leg that would be
easily managed under normal circumstances

00:06:49.340 --> 00:06:52.900
becomes that much worse when there aren’t
enough people to help, aren’t enough beds

00:06:52.900 --> 00:06:54.479
to go around.

00:06:54.479 --> 00:06:58.550
The more people who are exposed, the more
need to roll the dice against that one to

00:06:58.550 --> 00:07:02.159
two percent, and the more are going to lose.

00:07:02.159 --> 00:07:06.639
As of March 2020 most of the United States
and Canada have entered a period of uncertain

00:07:06.639 --> 00:07:07.650
quarantining.

00:07:07.650 --> 00:07:11.639
Non-essential businesses are closed, events
are canceled, workers are being sent home

00:07:11.639 --> 00:07:17.130
or laid off, borders are being shut down,
and the economy is in freefall.

00:07:17.130 --> 00:07:22.110
Every existing societal problem, from income
inequality to housing inequality to healthcare,

00:07:22.110 --> 00:07:28.539
is being stressed and amplified by not only
the virus but the complicity of our governments.

00:07:28.539 --> 00:07:32.870
News comes out hourly about warnings the people
in charge received months ago, and the ways

00:07:32.870 --> 00:07:36.870
in which they were either ignored or exploited
for personal gain.

00:07:36.870 --> 00:07:40.919
Several American politicians were briefed
on the security risks of COVID 19 in late

00:07:40.919 --> 00:07:45.460
January, and then took to Twitter to decry
public fear as a partisan hoax while they

00:07:45.460 --> 00:07:49.930
dumped their stocks in preparation for a crash
that they knew was coming.

00:07:49.930 --> 00:07:53.960
People in government, their corporate donors,
and their pundit allies are getting anxious,

00:07:53.960 --> 00:07:59.310
debating breaking quarantine and telling everyone
to go back to work and roll the dice on whether

00:07:59.310 --> 00:08:02.400
or not they’re going to die for the economy.

00:08:02.400 --> 00:08:06.440
We are standing on the precipice of a very
uncertain future, and we don’t know if that

00:08:06.440 --> 00:08:10.539
future is days, weeks, months, or years away.

00:08:10.539 --> 00:08:14.320
This could be the new normal for a very long
time.

00:08:14.320 --> 00:08:17.729
So why do I keep watching Contagion?

00:08:17.729 --> 00:08:22.610
A dimension of narrative that I like to bring
up pretty regularly is the idea that one purpose

00:08:22.610 --> 00:08:28.210
of fiction is that it allows us a space to
practice intense emotions and states without

00:08:28.210 --> 00:08:33.030
exposing us to the complexities or harms of
those states in reality.

00:08:33.030 --> 00:08:38.610
This is typically in the context of the fanciful:
reckless stunts, wild sex, gun fights, or

00:08:38.610 --> 00:08:40.200
general risky behaviour.

00:08:40.200 --> 00:08:45.820
We talked about this with Fifty Shades and
the idea of non-consent as a fantasy subject.

00:08:45.820 --> 00:08:51.730
Watching a disaster film in a disaster, particularly
one as sociologically driven as Contagion,

00:08:51.730 --> 00:08:53.790
is an extension of this.

00:08:53.790 --> 00:08:59.220
Rather than practicing intense emotional states
before they happen, this instinct of exposing

00:08:59.220 --> 00:09:04.980
ourselves to what we’re already experiencing,
amplifying existing emotional states, it works

00:09:04.980 --> 00:09:08.040
as a form of emotional inoculation.

00:09:08.040 --> 00:09:14.040
I am scared and anxious and uncertain, and
so I will make myself more scared and more

00:09:14.040 --> 00:09:19.420
anxious and more uncertain, because it’s
still fiction, it’s still safe, it still

00:09:19.420 --> 00:09:21.230
has an end.

00:09:21.230 --> 00:09:23.540
It is bounded.

00:09:23.540 --> 00:09:27.350
Things will get bad, things will then get
worse, people will die.

00:09:27.350 --> 00:09:31.810
The world is unfair, it is unbalanced, it
is unjust, and catastrophe will bring out

00:09:31.810 --> 00:09:34.690
both the best and worst of all of us.

00:09:34.690 --> 00:09:37.160
And then it will end.

00:09:37.160 --> 00:09:39.730
Is there looting, and arson, and murder?

00:09:39.730 --> 00:09:40.730
Yeah.

00:09:40.730 --> 00:09:42.710
But it is, ultimately, out of the ordinary.

00:09:42.710 --> 00:09:47.780
People get paranoid, people get desperate,
they riot under stress, but even when food

00:09:47.780 --> 00:09:52.580
supply lines break down, the world isn’t
summarily turned over to those with the bullets

00:09:52.580 --> 00:09:54.730
and the willingness to use them.

00:09:54.730 --> 00:10:01.600
There is no Mad Max dystopia, no Fallout post-apocalypse,
because at the end of the day humans are pro-social.

00:10:01.600 --> 00:10:03.770
The cooperative survive.

00:10:03.770 --> 00:10:09.630
In 1349, in the midst of the black death,
it must have looked like the end of the world.

00:10:09.630 --> 00:10:14.930
Entire households, entire villages, dying
a gross, horrifying, painful death, month

00:10:14.930 --> 00:10:16.920
after month after month.

00:10:16.920 --> 00:10:22.350
Then for generations, every year wondering
if this was the year the plague returned.

00:10:22.350 --> 00:10:27.060
Was this the year there would be no one left
to bury the dead.

00:10:27.060 --> 00:10:28.640
But people survived.

00:10:28.640 --> 00:10:32.940
The working class, who bore the brunt of the
disease and saw the bodies of their families,

00:10:32.940 --> 00:10:38.080
clans, and communities piled like cordwood,
fought back against the aristocrats who isolated

00:10:38.080 --> 00:10:40.290
themselves in their towers and remote estates.

00:10:40.290 --> 00:10:47.070
It was messy, and bloody, and it took decades,
but in the end serfdom was abolished.

00:10:47.070 --> 00:10:51.470
Europe lost upwards of sixty percent of its
population over the course of five years,

00:10:51.470 --> 00:10:53.410
but it wasn’t armageddon.

00:10:53.410 --> 00:10:58.610
Things kept going, people kept going, and
Europe would go on to be absolute bastards

00:10:58.610 --> 00:11:00.540
to the rest of the world.

00:11:00.540 --> 00:11:06.350
The disease in Contagion is not unrealistic,
real diseases have been as deadly, or worse,

00:11:06.350 --> 00:11:08.280
but it is dramatic.

00:11:08.280 --> 00:11:14.200
It moves very, very quickly, is highly contagious,
and kills a huge number of those who are infected.

00:11:14.200 --> 00:11:19.120
In reality this aggressiveness would kinda
work against the disease, and, morbidly, would

00:11:19.120 --> 00:11:21.320
help responders limit the spread.

00:11:21.320 --> 00:11:25.490
It moves so fast and kills so quickly that
there’s little question of who has it, and

00:11:25.490 --> 00:11:30.020
within a couple days everyone who has it is
either recovered or dead.

00:11:30.020 --> 00:11:34.520
This was the aspect of the SARS epidemic that
allowed response teams to effectively quarantine

00:11:34.520 --> 00:11:37.090
the virus where it burnt itself out.

00:11:37.090 --> 00:11:42.730
That said it’s not impossible that something
could spread so aggressively, be so incredibly

00:11:42.730 --> 00:11:47.410
contagious, that it could spread like wildfire
and become almost impossible to contain before

00:11:47.410 --> 00:11:50.140
anyone even knows what’s going on.

00:11:50.140 --> 00:11:53.630
But it’s undeniably dramatic and emotionally
effective.

00:11:53.630 --> 00:11:55.130
48 hours.

00:11:55.130 --> 00:11:57.820
We can contain two days in our head.

00:11:57.820 --> 00:12:02.320
A situation where things will get materially
worse literally tomorrow or the day after

00:12:02.320 --> 00:12:08.180
if nothing is done right this second, that’s
a comprehensible timeline.

00:12:08.180 --> 00:12:12.440
Forty eight hours is short enough that in
a catastrophe, driven by adrenaline and stress

00:12:12.440 --> 00:12:17.180
and necessity, you can stay awake that long
without even realizing it.

00:12:17.180 --> 00:12:19.680
COVID’s life cycle is closer to a month.

00:12:19.680 --> 00:12:23.260
By the time you get sick you’ve already
been sick for two weeks, and now you’re

00:12:23.260 --> 00:12:25.630
in for hell for another two to four weeks.

00:12:25.630 --> 00:12:29.560
It’s just past the range where it really
feels real.

00:12:29.560 --> 00:12:34.630
Two weeks isn’t long, but it’s still over
the line into the indeterminate “future”.

00:12:34.630 --> 00:12:36.430
This problem extends in both directions.

00:12:36.430 --> 00:12:40.170
There’s only so much space in the mind for
time.

00:12:40.170 --> 00:12:44.410
As the news ramps up, as things get worse,
the present crowds out history.

00:12:44.410 --> 00:12:48.420
The distance between the irrelevant past and
the now contracts.

00:12:48.420 --> 00:12:50.920
’Days ago’ becomes distant.

00:12:50.920 --> 00:12:52.750
‘Months ago’ is irrelevant.

00:12:52.750 --> 00:12:54.600
Years ago is ancient.

00:12:54.600 --> 00:12:59.860
By evening even earlier the same day is suspect
in its relevance to the Now.

00:12:59.860 --> 00:13:04.670
We remember January but it has as much presence
in the mind as childhood.

00:13:04.670 --> 00:13:08.930
Our lives become super liminal, displaced
from time, as we wrestle with our own minds

00:13:08.930 --> 00:13:12.300
and how they try to process the chronology
of our own existence.

00:13:12.300 --> 00:13:16.820
By Sunday, Friday no longer feels real, and
yet every day’s news is the consequence

00:13:16.820 --> 00:13:20.820
of decisions made fourteen, twenty one, twenty
eight days ago.

00:13:20.820 --> 00:13:24.790
Today’s responses won’t yield results
until well into next month.

00:13:24.790 --> 00:13:29.460
This flaw in our meat is a gap into which
charlatans, hucksters, and conmen can drive

00:13:29.460 --> 00:13:32.840
a wedge and pry us open, and pry they will
try.

00:13:32.840 --> 00:13:37.300
When I first saw Contagion in 2012 I thought
the weakest element was what I considered

00:13:37.300 --> 00:13:40.330
at the time to be the demonization of online
media.

00:13:40.330 --> 00:13:44.390
Jude Law’s character plays an online pundit
and conspiracy theorist who preaches to an

00:13:44.390 --> 00:13:49.190
audience of millions about an herbal tincture
of forsythia that he claims is the cure, a

00:13:49.190 --> 00:13:52.020
cure he just-so-happens to be selling.

00:13:52.020 --> 00:13:56.370
It is, in 2020, the realest element of the
film.

00:13:56.370 --> 00:14:01.300
Herbal cures, hydrogen water, steam treatment,
teas, magnets, suspensions of silver, tinctures,

00:14:01.300 --> 00:14:02.300
and tonics.

00:14:02.300 --> 00:14:06.060
We’ve got pastors standing at the pulpit
telling their congregation it's all a hoax,

00:14:06.060 --> 00:14:10.370
that there’s no reason to suspend services,
that their nebulous enemies are just trying

00:14:10.370 --> 00:14:12.120
to shut them down.

00:14:12.120 --> 00:14:16.260
We now live in a world where the US president
told people based on a rumour that chloroquine,

00:14:16.260 --> 00:14:21.620
a drug used for treating malaria and lupus,
was the cure, so a man in Arizona ate a packet

00:14:21.620 --> 00:14:24.180
of fish tank cleaner containing the chemical.

00:14:24.180 --> 00:14:26.370
He’s dead now.

00:14:26.370 --> 00:14:28.310
And that is, again, all part of it.

00:14:28.310 --> 00:14:33.390
There is an escapism to a story about horrible
things, because that story is complete.

00:14:33.390 --> 00:14:34.510
It is bounded.

00:14:34.510 --> 00:14:38.230
It provides a framework to horror that doesn’t
exist in the real present.

00:14:38.230 --> 00:14:42.700
Our future is uncertain, beset on all sides
by devils, and we can come out better or we

00:14:42.700 --> 00:14:46.950
can come out worse or we can die and none
of us knows which it will be and we’re all

00:14:46.950 --> 00:14:53.040
screaming at those in power to make the moral
choice, to choose better.

00:14:53.040 --> 00:14:55.540
And I am in an absolute haze.

00:14:55.540 --> 00:14:58.240
My daily life has not much been impacted,
overtly.

00:14:58.240 --> 00:15:03.350
I’m already an agoraphobic shut-in who works
online and has a bad sleep schedule.

00:15:03.350 --> 00:15:04.750
But it’s too much.

00:15:04.750 --> 00:15:05.940
I’m tired all the time.

00:15:05.940 --> 00:15:10.300
I can’t pay attention to the news and I
can’t not pay attention to the news.

00:15:10.300 --> 00:15:11.300
Working is difficult.

00:15:11.300 --> 00:15:14.140
I have a long history of respiratory illness.

00:15:14.140 --> 00:15:16.350
I am at risk.

00:15:16.350 --> 00:15:21.390
On one hand I am deeply privileged to be in
a position where I am and can remain isolated.

00:15:21.390 --> 00:15:26.690
On the other hand I can’t even think about
the other hand.

00:15:26.690 --> 00:15:31.500
Disease does not have a narrative meaning,
it does not have an eye for poetry or twists

00:15:31.500 --> 00:15:32.730
or closure.

00:15:32.730 --> 00:15:35.250
The only meaning is in how we respond.

00:15:35.250 --> 00:15:40.260
So I watch Contagion over and over and over
again.

00:15:40.260 --> 00:15:44.810
Because I need to practice emotions, and I
need to live in a bounded world, and I need

00:15:44.810 --> 00:15:47.790
to believe we can choose better.

